"kind":"parenthetical","label":"atac:buchwalter_coaching_2019","identifier":"atac:buchwalter_coaching_2019","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Buchwalter ","key":"d6u1qTuf1Z"},{"type":"emphasis","children":[{"type":"text","value":"et al.","key":"jfi285NFaw"}],"key":"sRku1IFXyS"},{"type":"text","value":", 2019","key":"mLQ0TOmKeQ"}],"enumerator":"2","key":"Q4O4q2TdJ6"}],"key":"ZDHBjhnxBx"},{"type":"text","value":" over chromatin remodelers and histone modifications to transcription factors that displace nucleosomes and the methylation of ","position":{"start":{"line":1,"column":1},"end":{"line":1,"column":1}},"key":"dajEB4KgJZ"},{"type":"crossReference","label":"CpG","identifier":"term-cpg","children":[{"type":"text","value":"CpG","key":"p48Gz3Z0mT"}],"kind":"definitionTerm","template":"{name}","resolved":true,"html_id":"term-cpg","remote":true,"url":"/glossary","dataUrl":"/glossary.json","key":"Z4P2IiaoOD"},{"type":"text","value":" sites.\nFor a review of how these mechanisms regulate the genome, we refer to ","position":{"start":{"line":1,"column":1},"end":{"line":1,"column":1}},"key":"MiVf9cApPG"},{"type":"citeGroup","kind":"narrative","children":[{"type":"cite","kind":"narrative","label":"atac:isbel_generating_2022","identifier":"atac:isbel_generating_2022","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Isbel ","key":"jpU8ToY2qv"},{"type":"emphasis","children":[{"type":"text","value":"et al.","key":"BpTYVi1SsA"}],"key":"jQQSjIVLgv"},{"type":"text","value":" (2022)","key":"Zfb32v5ohd"}],"enumerator":"3","key":"ELLmXYD0fm"}],"key":"eFlKmsfhS7"},{"type":"text","value":".","position":{"start":{"line":1,"column":1},"end":{"line":1,"column":1}},"key":"WbIsyIDIc2"}],"key":"TReiji3PIL"}],"key":"AtSbsbnvvq"},{"type":"block","kind":"notebook-content","children":[{"type":"heading","depth":2,"position":{"start":{"line":1,"column":1},"end":{"line":1,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Experimental assay","position":{"start":{"line":1,"column":1},"end":{"line":1,"column":1}},"key":"B4DnsteuDi"}],"identifier":"experimental-assay","label":"Experimental assay","html_id":"experimental-assay","implicit":true,"key":"mJRqO4gwRt"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":3,"column":1},"end":{"line":7,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"The Assay for Transposase-Accessible Chromatin with sequencing (ATAC-seq) uses the Tn5 transposase, which inserts sequencing adapters into accessible chromatin and thereby cuts it into fragments.\nOnly fragments flanked by two different adapters are amplified, sequenced paired-end and aligned to the genome.\nIn single-cell protocols such as 10x scATAC-seq, the transposition is performed on isolated nuclei in bulk before each nucleus is barcoded in a droplet.\nThe 10x Multiome assay additionally barcodes the RNA of each nucleus, which measures chromatin accessibility and gene expression in the same cells.\nNuclei are profiled instead of whole cells, so the RNA part of Multiome data measures nuclear transcripts.","position":{"start":{"line":3,"column":1},"end":{"line":3,"column":1}},"key":"ybzMEDdoKF"}],"key":"DrkGtqRNxI"}],"key":"EXyVt3a0r8"},{"type":"block","kind":"notebook-content","children":[{"type":"heading","depth":2,"position":{"start":{"line":2,"column":1},"end":{"line":2,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Data characteristics","position":{"start":{"line":2,"column":1},"end":{"line":2,"column":1}},"key":"D0yO4tXyAY"}],"label":"chromatin-accessibility-introduction-key-takeaway-2","identifier":"chromatin-accessibility-introduction-key-takeaway-2","html_id":"chromatin-accessibility-introduction-key-takeaway-2","key":"ZnX6SybHvE"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":4,"column":1},"end":{"line":5,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Accessibility is measured across the whole genome, including non-coding regions, so there are no predefined features like genes in scRNA-seq.\nFragments are counted either in regions of high accessibility, called peaks, or in fixed-width tiles across the genome, which do not require peaks to be called first and cluster cells equally well ","position":{"start":{"line":4,"column":1},"end":{"line":4,"column":1}},"key":"RL5cGG2B53"},{"type":"citeGroup","kind":"parenthetical","children":[{"type":"cite","kind":"parenthetical","label":"atac:luo2024benchmarking","identifier":"atac:luo2024benchmarking","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Luo ","key":"aurAfAKMsc"},{"type":"emphasis","children":[{"type":"text","value":"et al.","key":"VEh3hfM1bh"}],"key":"qA0MssdQ4s"},{"type":"text","value":", 2024","key":"TpBKGMmv6O"}],"enumerator":"4","key":"xVNI7xcjJn"}],"key":"fDjCAa0eNT"},{"type":"text","value":".","position":{"start":{"line":4,"column":1},"end":{"line":4,"column":1}},"key":"XURsrdHM3R"}],"key":"oX8upY0Tgy"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":7,"column":1},"end":{"line":9,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"A diploid cell has only two copies of each position, and only 1-10% of accessible regions are detected per cell, which makes the data very sparse ","position":{"start":{"line":7,"column":1},"end":{"line":7,"column":1}},"key":"CgOchW2QT5"},{"type":"citeGroup","kind":"parenthetical","children":[{"type":"cite","kind":"parenthetical","label":"atac:chen_assessment_2019","identifier":"atac:chen_assessment_2019","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Chen ","key":"uyEuI20xli"},{"type":"emphasis","children":[{"type":"text","value":"et al.","key":"PTmcm06Z9R"}],"key":"SMX4VMkyKZ"},{"type":"text","value":", 2019","key":"Jd7nguorXG"}],"enumerator":"5","key":"jpwGDu49d0"}],"key":"nVovgrxYgY"},{"type":"text","value":".\nMany methods therefore binarize counts, but treating counts quantitatively improves model fit, clustering, cell type identification and batch integration ","position":{"start":{"line":7,"column":1},"end":{"line":7,"column":1}},"key":"XDSikt5fn2"},{"type":"citeGroup","kind":"parenthetical","children":[{"type":"cite","kind":"parenthetical","label":"atac:martens2024"